 "design": "R6 the cheap design a practitioner can afford, priced on the noisier of the two scales.\n\nDesign fixed before running (2026-09-16, written 19:15 EDT). snap-r6-two-runs-only crossed ten, fifteen\nand 25 recipes with two, three and four runs on a margin-like population, and the appendix now tells a\nreader to budget for recipes before runs because coverage held between 0.936 and 0.949 everywhere while\npower ran from 0.266 at the cheapest corner to 0.997 at 25 recipes by four. That advice was measured on\none scale. snap-r6-cluster-count-acc has since found that the accuracy battery needs fifteen recipes\nrather than ten, since the wild interval covers 0.869 at ten and 0.943 at fifteen at its item noise, so\nthe cheapest corner of the margin grid may not be a design a reader on the accuracy scale can use at all.\nThis run prices the same grid at the accuracy battery's correlation of 0.018 and item noise of 1.45, and\nit adds twenty recipes to the sweep because the floor the cluster sweep found sits between ten and\nfifteen. It scores ten, fifteen, twenty and 25 recipes against two, three and four runs at 4,000\nreplicates per cell, reporting coverage, the undefined rate, the exclusion rate at the true 1.078 and the\nmedian width. The cell that decides the advice is ten recipes with two runs, where the margin grid still\ncovered 0.936, and the paper should say plainly whether that design is usable at this noise. Seed\n20260993 keeps the draws distinct.\n",
